details.image_prompt:
{
  "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two simple, round, matte ceramic bowls filled with a vibrant, velvety bright green edamame and basil soup, centered and captured from a 3/4 overhead angle (40°). One bowl is in the sharp foreground, the second bowl is slightly recessed and softly blurred in the background. Beside each bowl, a slice of rustic, hearty whole-grain toast with a crisp, slightly uneven browned crust rests on the surface. The soup is topped with a scattering of raw, unsalted green pumpkin seeds, which are dispersed naturally and unevenly across the surface. The surface is a warm, aged oak wood table with visible grain and a few scattered crumbs from the toasted bread and a stray fresh basil leaf. Lighting is soft, natural side light from a window on the left, creating gentle shadows and highlighting the smooth, opaque texture of the pureed soup. A wooden spoon rests casually on the table near the foreground bowl. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: a tiny splash mark on the inner rim of one bowl, slightly unevenly shaped toasted bread slices. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a few stray pumpkin seeds on the wood grain. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting, not cold or clinical. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
}
